  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iran struck Bahrain, Kuwait, and Jordan on July 19, killing at least two US service members in Jordan and damaging infrastructure across the region. The IRGC issued a formal statement pledging further strikes on "new enemy bases in the region," citing US forward deployment of additional aircraft and refueling tankers to the Gulf. Kata'ib Hezbollah claimed separate attacks on Kuwait infrastructure on July 18 and July 19. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>The IRGC formally declared the Strait of Hormuz closed indefinitely on July 12, following the collapse of the MOU-derived shipping corridor arrangement established at Bürgenstock on June 20–22. Approximately 490 vessels anchored or idled in the broader Gulf region as of July 21. Daily commercial transits through the Strait stood at approximately 10 on July 20, compared to a pre-war baseline of approximately 94 transits per day. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>US Navy and coalition vessels enforced a naval blockade of Iranian ports beginning July 19, following what CENTCOM described as retaliation for the July 19 strikes on US personnel in Jordan. Houthi forces in Yemen declared a naval blockade of Saudi Arabia on July 20, following Saudi strikes on Sanaa Airport, with subsequent Houthi strikes confirmed at Abha International Airport. Dual-chokepoint disruption — Hormuz and the Bab el-Mandeb corridor — affects approximately 27% of globally traded oil as of July 21. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>Qatar, Egypt, and Pakistan presented a joint 10-day ceasefire proposal to both Washington and Tehran on July 21. Iranian Foreign Ministry spokesperson Esmail Baghaei confirmed receipt of the mediator proposals without acceptance language. Trump had not accepted the proposal as of end-of-day July 21; Axios reported the White House was assembling additional fighter aircraft and refueling tankers in the Gulf rather than standing down operations. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset passed a dissolution vote 62-0 on July 17, with Netanyahu voting in favor; elections are formally set for October 27, 2026. The July 21 Haaretz/Channel 12 poll modeled the opposition bloc at 69 seats versus the outgoing coalition at 51, with Eisenkot leading Netanyahu as preferred prime minister 46% to 36%. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

